# Version

## Current Version

`0.12.0`

## Version Type

Minor package-boundary and examples-first architecture milestone over `0.11.0`.

This version implements Phase 5 bidirectional NL <-> DSL support for the deterministic, mock-safe Intent/Contract planner boundary. `@office-dsl/llm-planner` now defines a controlled `intent-contract.planner-response.v1` response schema for OpenRouter-style output, validates malformed responses before DSL creation, converts valid structured responses into canonical `intent-contract.dsl.v1`, plans varied single-message natural language and guideline-file text into sourced DSL fields, renders DSL back to traceable natural-language summary statements, and includes round-trip regression checks that prevent rendered NL from introducing unauthorized field paths.

The Phase 5 implementation is validated offline. It does not call OpenRouter during default verification and does not claim production semantic extraction from arbitrary documents; the controlled schema and prompt boundary are ready for an online provider to use behind explicit configuration.

This version also implements Phase 6 contract and legal document renderers in the new `@office-dsl/document-renderer` package. The renderers turn an `intent-contract.dsl.v1` snapshot into task delegation, service agreement, and employment/guideline draft documents, embed a draft legal disclaimer in every output, render missing/ambiguous/conflicting/unapproved fields as explicit gaps, and attach a document-to-DSL traceability map. The renderers are deterministic and read-only over the DSL and never invent terms. Wiring them into the runtime, CLI, backend, and UI, and gating rendering behind bilateral canonical approval, remains open.

This version also implements Phase 8 test generation in the new `@office-dsl/testgen` package. It defines typed `intent-contract.testgen-input.v1` inputs (`REQUIREMENT`, `INVARIANT`, `ACCEPTANCE_CRITERIA`, `PROHIBITED_BEHAVIOR`, `EXPECTED_OUTPUT`, `ERROR_HANDLING`, `SECURITY_POLICY`), extracts them deterministically from an Intent/Contract DSL snapshot, generates unit/integration/API/E2E/security/error-handling test specifications mapped back to DSL inputs, and verifies coverage against acceptance criteria with uncovered criteria surfaced in the verifier input. The generator is deterministic and read-only over the DSL; it does not execute generated code and does not create tests from loose prompt text. Runtime and Python-verifier integration remain open.

This version also implements Phase 9 JS/Node.js code generation in the new `@office-dsl/codegen` package. It defines a bounded dependency-free Node ESM target, requires bilateral Human1/Human2 approval of the current Intent/Contract DSL hash before generation, emits a deterministic implementation plan, generates a package manifest, contract-spec module, and generated Node.js test file, runs that generated test file in a temporary workspace, checks generated source for forbidden dynamic/network/process APIs, verifies file hashes, and returns `codegen.verifier-input.v1` with generated file metadata and test results. Code generation is still a package-level deterministic boundary; runtime, CLI, backend, UI, and Python-verifier integration remain open.

This version also implements the mock-safe Phase 10 Python semantic verifier boundary. `verify_semantic` accepts structured semantic input, detects missing required DSL fields, unresolved conflicts, unapproved assumptions, source quotes absent from original NL, rendered-document mismatches with DSL paths, failed generated code tests, and uncovered generated-test acceptance criteria. The TypeScript runtime can invoke `python -m office_dsl_verifier --semantic-input ...`, capture `semantic-verifier.report.v1` in audit, and gate failed or review-needed sessions at `VERIFICATION_FAILED`. LiteLLM/OpenRouter semantic verifier mode is implemented behind explicit configuration and validated by default with a monkeypatched provider adapter plus an opt-in live smoke test.


This version also extracts package boundaries that were previously embedded in runners or CLI entrypoints. Chat negotiation now lives in `@office-dsl/chat-negotiation`, recruitment orchestration in `@office-dsl/recruitment-workflow`, deterministic document ingestion/OCR fixture processing in `@office-dsl/document-ingestion`, DSL/HCL artifact writing in `@office-dsl/dsl-artifact-renderer`, regression comparison helpers in `@office-dsl/regression-runner`, Python verifier invocation in `@office-dsl/verifier-bridge`, and CLI mock verification in `@office-dsl/verifier-mock`. Workspace package/app versions are lockstep at `0.12.0`, internal source-relative package imports have been replaced with package-name imports, and `tests/package-boundary.test.ts` guards these boundaries.

This version also implements Phase 11 cross-platform CLI support. `packages/cli` adds a `well-manifest-intent` entry point (`packages/cli/src/intent.ts`) that exposes canonical Intent/Contract commands (`plan`, `plan-file`, `render`, `testgen`, `codegen`, `verify`, `approve`, `chat`, `example`, `recruitment`, `version`, `help`) with `--json` and `--human` output modes. A `packages/cli/src/cross-platform.ts` helper module normalizes POSIX/Windows paths and `file://` URLs for both operating systems, and `project.bat` provides the same validation workflow as `./project.sh` on Windows. The canonical CLI commands are implemented but are not yet fully integrated with runtime approval gates, backend endpoints, or UI flows.

